# Code owners are automatically requested for review on matching paths, and —
# when "Require review from Code Owners" is enabled in branch protection — must
# approve before a PR can merge. See https://docs.github.com/articles/about-code-owners
#
# List ALL owners for a pattern on a SINGLE line — CODEOWNERS is last-match-wins,
# so multiple "* ..." lines do NOT combine (only the last would apply). Valid
# owners are GitHub usernames (@user), teams (@org/team), or emails. A bare org
# handle (@collabdt) or a display name with spaces (@Collab Digital Twins) is not
# valid and makes GitHub ignore the file.
#
# The team needs explicit **Write** access to this repository or every rule here is
# silently ignored. Org ownership is not enough; grant the team the repo. Open this
# file in the GitHub web UI once after changing it — that view is the only place
# GitHub reports an unknown user, a typo, or a team lacking write access.

* @CollabDigitalTwins/maintainers

# Below: last-match-wins, so these override the catch-all for their paths. Same owner
# today, listed separately because they are release decisions rather than code changes
# and should stay owned even if `*` is ever delegated. See CLAUDE.md "Releasing core".

# The version bump and the public release notes: together these are the release.
/package.json     @CollabDigitalTwins/maintainers
/CHANGELOG.md     @CollabDigitalTwins/maintainers

# Workflows hold the publish credentials, so editing one is equivalent to holding them.
/.github/         @CollabDigitalTwins/maintainers

# The CI guards themselves. Editing these disables the checks that protect everything else.
/scripts/check-*.mjs   @CollabDigitalTwins/maintainers
